# pokezero gen3 fidelity fix: the end-of-turn residual block STOPS when a faint
# ends the battle. The engine ran it to completion.
#
# GROUND TRUTH, read at source before touching anything. `Battle.fieldEvent`
# (sim/battle.ts) is the residual dispatch loop:
#
#   while (handlers.length) {
#       const handler = handlers[0]; handlers.shift();
#       ...
#       if (handler.callback) { this.singleEvent(handlerEventid, effect, ...); }
#       this.faintMessages();
#       if (this.ended) return;      // <-- between entries
#   }
#
# The boundary is exact and it is BETWEEN entries, never mid-entry: the
# faint-causing entry runs to completion, `faintMessages()` resolves the faint and
# `checkWin` sets `ended`, and every REMAINING handler is skipped. The same early
# return guards the duration-expiry branch a few lines above, and `case 'residual'`
# suppresses even the `upkeep` line with `if (!this.ended)`.
#
# So the winner's own Leftovers fires or not purely by ORDER: order 5 resolves
# before a poison faint at order 9 and must still happen; a burn tick sharing the
# status-damage bracket but speed-sorted after it must not. Both directions are
# pinned, because a fix that truncates too early is as wrong as one that truncates
# too late.
#
# INTERACTION WITH THE DEFERRAL (poke-engine-gen3-residual-defer-on-faint.patch),
# verified rather than assumed: the two are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE.
# `end_of_turn_is_deferred` fires only when a side OWES A REPLACEMENT, and a side
# can only owe one while it still has a living Pokemon to send. If the faint ended
# the battle there is no replacement to owe, so the deferral cannot trigger; if a
# replacement is owed the battle is not over, so this guard cannot trigger. Pinned
# from both sides by deferral_and_battle_end_are_mutually_exclusive.
#
# The #888 double-wipe tie divergence is untouched and re-pinned here: gen3 Showdown
# ties a simultaneous last-mon faint, the engine awards it to side two. Still
# deliberately deferred, and now guarded against silent movement by this change.
#
# MECHANISM. A macro rather than a restructure, so the guard is a ONE-LINE insertion
# at each entry boundary instead of an 800-line re-indent of a vendored function: it
# performs the function's own apply/reverse bookkeeping before returning, exactly as
# the tail does. Inserted at the top of each per-side residual section body (nine of
# them) plus once on entry, which is every point at which control passes to the next
# residual entry.
#
# AMENDED by poke-engine-gen3-weather-entry-truncation.patch. The MECHANISM
# paragraph above contradicts the rationale paragraph before it, and the
# rationale is the correct one: the boundary is BETWEEN entries. Inserting the
# guard "at the top of each per-side residual section body" is right for order 10,
# where each Pokemon really is its own handler, and WRONG for order 8, where the
# weather chip is ONE Field-held entry damaging both actives. That mis-slice
# dropped the winner's chip and cost holdout rows 19100002/53 and 19100154/75; the
# later patch hoists the order-8 guard out of its loop. This paragraph is left in
# place rather than rewritten because it is the evidence for how the defect stayed
# invisible: a pin was written to match it. See reports/c122.
#
# Pinned by rust/pokezero-search/tests/gen3_battle_end_residuals.rs (three of its
# five tests fail on the tree without this patch). Touches
# gen3/generate_instructions.rs only; authored against the tree the list leaves.
